# One line per hunk, per the rule in capstone/tests/port-effort.py: file:new_start, class, level,
# and why. Classes are hierarchy, metadata, workaround; levels are allocator, application. The rule
# was fixed before the first ported line, in PREREGISTRATION.md.
#
# The split. Upstream keeps a refstruct object's RefCount in the object's own allocation, just below
# the user's pointer, and a pool's freelist link in that header (ref->opaque.nc). Both leave: every
# hunk that moves the header out, finds it again by address, or frees the header and the object as
# two allocations is metadata. Everything that takes a block, carves, takes, gives or revokes is
# hierarchy, as in 0001.
#
# Mixed hunks, and which way they were resolved. Three hunks carry lines of both kinds, and a hunk
# takes ONE class. Each is classed by what it is for, following 0001's precedent for the same shape,
# and each resolution goes AGAINST the phase's prediction (B3: metadata > hierarchy), so the split
# cannot have been tilted toward it:
#   318  pool_free and pool_free_entry: the revokes (destroy, take, give) plus rs_free_plain and
#        rs_index_del, 2 metadata lines                                   -> hierarchy
#   399  a new entry: carve and take, plus the header's allocation and index entry (0001's buffer.c:388,
#        which also allocates the entry's record, is hierarchy)          -> hierarchy
#   429  a failed init: the give, plus rs_index_del, 1 metadata line     -> hierarchy
#
# SCOPE. The count covers the patch only, as every port's .classes does. The Sublet calls themselves
# (sublet_carve/take/give, the heap's linear lend and free) live in the app port's
# src/capstone-domain/ffsublet.c and the interface in sublet/ffsublet.h, which are outside the patch.
# The results report the count with that file added as hierarchy as well, because the choice moves
# the split (the wmem port's audit, 2026-09-29, showed the same choice flipping its H4 outcome).
#
# No hunk is a workaround. No hunk is at the application level: every line is inside libavutil's
# refstruct implementation.

libavutil/refstruct.c:21    hierarchy   allocator   the level below's interface arrives
libavutil/refstruct.c:68    metadata    allocator   the header leaves the object: its fields for the object, its key and the index chain; the address index; the lookup that reads the pointer through its own authority first; a plain object's end frees both
libavutil/refstruct.c:194   metadata    allocator   a plain object is two allocations: the header beside it, the object alone and bounded to its size
libavutil/refstruct.c:307   metadata    allocator   the pool holds the list of blocks it took from the heap
libavutil/refstruct.c:318   hierarchy   allocator   the pool's end is one revoke per block; a freed entry is taken for its callback and given back (mixed: 2 metadata lines, see header)
libavutil/refstruct.c:350   hierarchy   allocator   an entry's return is a revoke, before the freelist can reissue or free it
libavutil/refstruct.c:386   hierarchy   allocator   a reissue is a take: a new handle, a new alias
libavutil/refstruct.c:399   hierarchy   allocator   a new entry is carved from the pool's block and handed out under its own handle, zeroed as upstream zeroes it (mixed: see header)
libavutil/refstruct.c:429   hierarchy   allocator   a failed init gives the entry back; its storage stays in the block until the pool's end (mixed: 1 metadata line)
libavutil/refstruct.c:508   hierarchy   allocator   the pool takes its entry size for the blocks it will carve
libavutil/refstruct.c:537   metadata    allocator   a pool that cannot be set up is freed as a plain object: header and object both
